{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>To go astray after Molech</b> — to include other forms of foreign worship that one served in this manner, even if this is not the normal mode of their worship; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 20:5). He also wrote earlier in the section of Aḥarei Mot (above Leviticus 18:21) that Molech is a foreign worship whose name is Molech, and this is its mode of worship, that one hands over one's child to the priests, and so on. But none of this is correct and sound according to the examination of the Gemara (Sanhedrin 64a), for according to the one who says Molech is foreign worship, the worship of that foreign worship is not through passing children to it, for if so, Scripture would not have needed to mention this at all, since it is already included in the warning against foreign worship, concerning which several general warnings come in the Torah, and it is also included in the punishment stated in the section \"If there be found among you, within any of your gates, man or woman who does what is evil in the eyes of HaShem your Elohim, to transgress His covenant, and goes and serves other elohim and bows down to them\" (Deuteronomy 17:2–3). Rather, perforce we must expound that the entire essence of the verse came only to impose liability for this worship of passing seed in a manner that is not its standard mode. And thus they said in the Gemara (Sanhedrin there): and according to the one who says Molech is foreign worship — why do I need the excision of Molech? For passing one's child not in its standard mode. And the same applies to the punishment and the warning stated concerning it, which were needed only for passing one's child not in its standard mode. And it appears that according to this opinion, the term \"Molech\" is not a name for a particular idol or image but a name for any worshipped entity in general — anything that you enthrone and accept upon yourself as a deity. And according to this opinion, the children of Ammon would call their abomination \"Molech\" (1 Kings 11:7), for it is their king, and it is a comprehensive name for anything worshipped, for it is from the root of kingship. And not like the words of the Rav z\"l, who said that it is a foreign worship whose name is \"Molech\" and its worship is through this. Likewise, what Rashi wrote here — that \"to go astray after Molech\" was needed to include other foreign worship that one served in this manner, even if this is not the mode of its worship — is also impossible for the reason we mentioned, since the entire point is that the excision in the case of Molech was stated only to impose liability where it is not its standard mode, and every foreign worship is included in that. Nor can one say that Scripture imposed excision for that foreign worship whose name is Molech when one passes to it not in its standard mode, and then went back and included Peor and Merkolīs in it for excision — for why would Scripture need to do so? What distinguishes that foreign worship from all other foreign worship in the world? Moreover, if that were so, Scripture would need to include as well passing one's child to Peor or to Merkolīs for stoning, just as it included it for excision. Rather, Scripture did not mention Molech at all — whether in the warning (above 18:21) or in the punishment of excision and stoning (here) — except for passing one's child not in its standard mode to any foreign worship in the world. And whether you say that Molech is a name for all foreign worship as we have explained, or whether you say that it is a specific known foreign worship that is called thus, Scripture mentioned that foreign worship regarding a mode not its own, and the same applies to all of them, for this stringency is on account of this severe act of worship. And the Gemara in tractate Sanhedrin (Sanhedrin 64a) proves all this according to the very opinion that the Rav wrote, that Molech is foreign worship. But our Mishnah differs, according to the one who says Molech is not foreign worship — that is, it is not worshipped as something accepted upon them as a deity at all, but is like an act of sorcery, to divine concerning the living to that dead dog. And according to this opinion, one who passes his child to Peor or to Merkolīs is exempt. Also, the words of Rashi in his commentary on Sanhedrin are as we have written. And the differing baraita in Torat Kohanim (Kedoshim 10:13), \"I will cut him off, and all who go astray after him, to go astray after Molech\" — to include other foreign worship in excision — does not derive this from the superfluity of \"to go astray after Molech\" as is apparent from the Rav's words; rather, it says that this entire excision is superfluous, and it is expounded for other foreign worship. And this is what they said in the Gemara (Sanhedrin 64a): three excisions for foreign worship — why? One for its standard mode; and one for a mode not its own — that is, for slaughtering, burning incense, libation, and prostration that were extended to a non-standard mode for the death penalty, and here it imposes liability for excision; and one for Molech. And according to the one who says Molech is foreign worship, it is for passing one's child not in its standard mode, that is, to any foreign worship in the world as we have mentioned."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {0 "Ramban's phrase לִדְרֹשׁ בְּעַד הַחַיִּים אֶל הַכֶּלֶב הַמֵּת הַהוּא (lit. 'to divine concerning the living to that dead dog') is an idiom drawn from the Gemara's analogy; rendered hyper-literally."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:57:13.658280633Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}